* ===========================================================================
* DOES FIELDMGR APPEND SURVIVE A LIVE CDX?  -- the index gap
* ===========================================================================
*
* A PROBE, NOT A REGISTERED SPEC. Not in kRegressionSpecs; runs by DOTSCRIPT.
*
* WHY. The 2026-08-31 catalog conversion proved FIELDMGR APPEND preserves memo
* tokens and never opens the memo sidecar -- measured on WORKSPACES, 273 rows,
* a 3.1 MB .dtx, all 20 original column hashes identical and the sidecar md5
* byte-identical. THAT TABLE HAD NO INDEX. dottalkpp\data\workspaces held only
* the .dbf and the .dtx: no .cdx, no .cnx.
*
* And cmd_fieldmgr.cpp sets both knobs OFF before calling fields::append --
*
*     opts.rebuildIndexesIfPossible = false;
*     opts.failIfIndexesPresent     = false;
*
* -- so on an indexed table the append NEITHER REBUILDS NOR REFUSES. The
* append changes the record length (703 -> 715 on the catalog) and grows the
* header (1266 -> 1381). Whether a CDX survives that is UNMEASURED, and those
* two flags are the written evidence that nobody has checked.
*
* THE CLAIM THIS GUARDS AGAINST. "FIELDMGR APPEND is safe" was about to be
* recorded as a general result about restructuring any x64 table. Almost every
* table in this tree carries an index; the one it was measured on does not.
* A result measured on the unindexed case and stated for all cases is the
* shape this house keeps finding.
*
* THREE POSSIBLE OUTCOMES, and they are NOT equally bad:
*
*   (a) the order still walks correctly    -- CDX keys on RECORD NUMBERS, not
*       byte offsets, so a reclen change need not disturb it. Best case.
*   (b) SET ORDER fails / refuses          -- loud. Recoverable. A user knows.
*   (c) SET ORDER is ACCEPTED and the walk goes PHYSICAL -- SILENT staleness,
*       and the worst of the three: a stale index that answers is worse than
*       one that refuses, because nothing says the answer changed.
*
* NO PREDICTION IS OFFERED because I do not have one. Stating a guess here
* would invite reading the result to match it.
*
* HOW IT ASSERTS. By FIELD VALUE, never console text, and the discriminator
* DIVERGES AT BOTH ENDS -- physical ZULU..TANGO against keyed ALPHA..ZULU.
* That is MWX section 5C's fixture and its reason: an engine that accepted
* SET ORDER and walked physically anyway would leave both ends where the
* pre-index guards found them, so a same-order key could not tell the two
* implementations apart.
*
* GUARDS. G1/G2 read PHYSICAL order before any index exists. G3/G4 read the
* KEYED order BEFORE the append, which is what makes T1/T2 mean anything:
* without them, a broken order after the append could not be told from an
* order that never worked. IF ANY GUARD READS .F., TREAT THE ARMS AS UNPROVEN.
*
* COUNT: 4 guards + 4 arms = 8 FMI_ lines. An errored marker prints NOTHING
* rather than going red, so a SHORT COUNT IS THE RESULT -- and for T1/T2 a
* missing line is outcome (b), which is itself the finding.
*
* Self-bootstrapping and self-erasing in DBF/SANDBOX. ERASE sweeps the table,
* the container and the LMDB environment together, so CDX CREATE -- which
* refuses to overwrite -- finds clean ground on a re-run.
* ===========================================================================

SETPATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX
SETPATH INDEXES INDEXES/SANDBOX

ERASE FMIDX CONFIRM

SELECT 0
CREATE X64 FMIDX (CK C(4), CLBL C(12))
APPEND
REPLACE CK WITH "C1"
REPLACE CLBL WITH "ZULU"
APPEND
REPLACE CK WITH "C2"
REPLACE CLBL WITH "MIKE"
APPEND
REPLACE CK WITH "C3"
REPLACE CLBL WITH "ALPHA"
APPEND
REPLACE CK WITH "C4"
REPLACE CLBL WITH "TANGO"

USE FMIDX
SELECT FMIDX
TOP
? "FMI_G1_physical_first_is_ZULU:" + (ALLTRIM(CLBL) = "ZULU")
BOTTOM
? "FMI_G2_physical_last_is_TANGO:" + (ALLTRIM(CLBL) = "TANGO")

CDX CREATE
CDX ADDTAG CLBL
CDX TAGS
BUILDLMDB YES

&& Re-open before ordering: the container and its LMDB environment did not
&& exist when this area was first opened (MWX 5C's own note).
USE FMIDX
SELECT FMIDX
SET ORDER TAG CLBL
TOP
? "FMI_G3_keyed_first_is_ALPHA_before_append:" + (ALLTRIM(CLBL) = "ALPHA")
BOTTOM
? "FMI_G4_keyed_last_is_ZULU_before_append:" + (ALLTRIM(CLBL) = "ZULU")

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* THE APPEND -- the whole point of the file
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

FIELDMGR APPEND FMNEW N(4)
FIELDMGR LIST

USE FMIDX
SELECT FMIDX
SET ORDER TAG CLBL
TOP
? "FMI_T1_keyed_first_still_ALPHA_after_append:" + (ALLTRIM(CLBL) = "ALPHA")
BOTTOM
? "FMI_T2_keyed_last_still_ZULU_after_append:" + (ALLTRIM(CLBL) = "ZULU")

&& T3 IS THE DATA ARM AND ITS FIRST VERSION WAS WORTHLESS -- recorded because
&& the comment above it already said the right thing and the code did not.
&& It read TOP, which DEPENDS ON THE ORDER APPLYING, so when SET ORDER was
&& refused it reported .F. for the same reason T1 and T2 did: it measured the
&& order, not the data. One marker CANNOT say which failed, and that was the
&& stated reason for having a separate arm at all.
&&
&& GO 3 is a PHYSICAL address and is immune to the thing under test. Under a
&& working order record 3 is ALPHA; under a refused order record 3 is still
&& ALPHA, because physical record 3 is ALPHA either way. That is the property
&& a data arm needs.
GO 3
? "FMI_T3_row3_data_intact_CK:" + (ALLTRIM(CK) = "C3")
? "FMI_T4_row3_data_intact_CLBL:" + (ALLTRIM(CLBL) = "ALPHA")
